WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.250
DAVID GLAZER: Hello,
everyone, and welcome

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.230
to med school-- or
not quite, but we're

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.730
going to be talking
today about how

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.770
the world of the cloud
and the world of bits

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.570
is being put to use in the world
of genomics of life science

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.780
in the world of medicine.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.140
And we have a handful
of speakers here

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.870
who are going to walk
you through a set of case

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.130
studies, a set of
examples, showing what

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.230
is it like, what does it mean,
what are the possibilities when

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.290
we take this world of the
cloud that you've been hearing

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.700
about for the last
two days and use it

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.710
in the world of life science,
the world of genomics,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.980
the world of working with
human data, medical data,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.650
biological data.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:59.670
And I am going to start with one
slide of biology 101, genomics

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.060
101, just to kind of orient you
to some of the different topics

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.580
and applications that
you're going to be hearing.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.890
So for those of you who
have background in this,

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.760
turn off your brain for
the next 60 seconds.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.350
For the rest of you,
here's a very quick intro.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.110
Pay attention,
because it's fast.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.650
So the rough sequence
of how genomics works

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.870
is you take tissue from a human,
you run it through a machine,

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.400
a sequencing machine-- those
machines cost about $1 million

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.640
apiece-- and they spit out
this 100-gigabyte file,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.980
which is all the raw
data from any one of us.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.040
You then take that
data, and now we're

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.340
in the world of computing.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.280
Now we're in the world
that we all live in.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.500
And you take that data
and you process it

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.280
through what's called secondary
analysis down to a smaller,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.084
more informative set of summary
information about who am I,

00:01:45.084 --> 00:01:46.750
how am I different
from you, how are you

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.880
different from you, like that.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.520
And then finally, you
get to the so-what stage.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.980
And the so-what
stage is now that we

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.490
know what it is that
makes me "me" genomically,

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:58.560
why does that matter?

00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:59.860
How can we put that to use?

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.090
How can we use that medically
to understand, to treat,

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.790
to reach conclusions and
insights and do interventions?

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:10.180
So that, in a very,
very quick pass,

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.700
is what the world of working
with genomic data looks like.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.780
You're going to hear
some examples of people

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.250
who do primary
analysis, and therefore

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.640
have a big need for
doing secondary analysis,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.872
and how the cloud is
helping them do it better.

00:02:22.872 --> 00:02:24.580
You're going to hear
examples from people

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.900
who are at the far end,
in the health care side,

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.950
and how they use all of this
genomic data to get there.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.720
And some examples
across the way.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.260
All right, so that
was the biology 101.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.590
Context for why this is the
right time for the cloud

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.586
and for biology and genomics
to be working together-- this

00:02:44.586 --> 00:02:46.460
is the slide the National
Institute of Health

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.500
has been maintaining for
the last couple of decades,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:55.480
and it shows a plummeting cost
of producing one human genome.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.450
It's a slide any of you in the
field have seen repeatedly.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.210
The takeaway here
is it's going down.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.310
Well, what generally
happens when prices go down

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.650
is volume goes up.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:09.894
And that is indeed
what's been happening.

00:03:09.894 --> 00:03:11.810
There was an article
that came out last summer

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:12.930
with a catchy title.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.600
They said that the
word "astronomical"

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.660
is an all-purpose adjective
to mean a lot of stuff.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.790
It's kind of passe.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.400
Astronomical isn't
the big anymore.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.640
The word for "big"
should be "genomical,"

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.350
because they did some analysis
and they did some projections

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.946
and said, that's a log scale.

00:03:28.946 --> 00:03:30.320
So if you look
over on the right,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.900
you're seeing it growing from
peta-basepairs to exa- to zeta.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.120
These are numbers that are
pretty big, and getting bigger.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.340
The other thing I want to
point out on this chart

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.570
is if you look right
about in the middle,

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.920
there's that dot
labeled 1,000 genomes,

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.280
and around 2011 or '12.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.910
That was a very famous big
study that was very impressive.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.790
The world was excited to see
that, hey, we've analyzed

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:56.630
1,000 genomes in one place.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.570
And people learned
a lot from that.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:02.530
And 1,000 already today sounds
like a very small number.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.850
So the feeling of
working with this data

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.570
is changing qualitatively.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.590
That's what happens
on log scales.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:16.079
So with dropping costs, with
accelerating amounts of data--

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:18.540
with accelerating amounts of
data that mean accelerating

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.630
not just more data but more
insights, because it's things

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.320
you can't learn from just
looking at 100 or 1,000 people.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.210
You can only learn them when
you look at tens of thousands

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:29.650
and hundreds of thousands.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:33.290
In that world, fortunately
the world of cloud computing

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.460
has been building the
tools to solve the problem

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:38.980
at the same time as the
world of life science

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.350
has been building the tools
to create the problem.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:44.090
And you're going to hear
today some examples of how

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.170
this works together.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.550
One example of a
project that's ongoing

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.370
that actually three
of the speakers

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:55.320
here are involved with with
us is the National Cancer

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.780
Institute has
spent, when does it

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.380
say, last five or
six years-- no,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.120
a decade actually, last
decade-- assembling

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.650
a very interesting database
of cancer genomic data.

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.160
And they have lots of different
samples of cancer, of tumor

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.620
cells and normal cells.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.990
And they've analyzed it,
and they have all this data.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.870
And it's basically been
sitting on an FTP site

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:21.350
with a terabyte of data, which
is not the friendliest way

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.580
to analyze this
really valuable asset.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:24.770
And they know this.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:29.180
And they created a project to
say, let's put this out and see

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:32.700
if anyone can take our data
and put it in the cloud

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.380
and make it useful in the cloud.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.780
They awarded three
grants to do that.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.061
The three grants went to
three of the institutions who

00:05:39.061 --> 00:05:40.310
are going to be speaking here.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.310
And you'll hear a little
bit about what they are

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.650
doing with this information.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:47.850
But this is an example of one
of the first projects that's

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.920
taking valuable data at scale,
putting it in the cloud,

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.650
and making it useful
for researchers.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:54.790
There's a few.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:56.290
There's going to be many more.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.190
And you're going to hear a few
speakers talking about this.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.420
This is the context for that.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.860
Why do this in the cloud, right?

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:06.830
Why?

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:07.650
It's just software.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:08.920
We're just analyzing stuff.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.014
Well, the reason is
because data is data,

00:06:12.014 --> 00:06:13.430
and a lot of the
tools that you've

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.600
been hearing about for
the last couple of days

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.300
turn out to be very
valuable and very

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.662
relevant to solving the problems
of genomics data in the cloud.

00:06:21.662 --> 00:06:23.370
So you're going to
see examples of people

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.340
who are standing on
the shoulders of all

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.030
the tools, both general
purpose infrastructure

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.240
and dedicated
infrastructure for genomics,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.510
and building solutions on top.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:34.690
Many of them are
working with the tools

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.020
that Google builds that
are dedicated for genomics.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:42.690
Many of them are using general
purpose compute platform tools.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.940
In the world of Google
Genomics, the rough data flow--

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.790
take the data off
that sequencer,

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:51.260
load it into the cloud, import
it into Google Genomics,

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.640
and export it, with
the goal of making

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.880
it available to lots
of different audiences

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.460
to do their analysis,
do their work.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.090
The four speakers
you're going to hear

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:03.910
are from these institutions,
from the Broad Institute, ISB,

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.706
Seven Bridges, and Stanford.

00:07:05.706 --> 00:07:07.330
And the speakers in
particular-- you're

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:09.690
going to be hearing from
Kris Cibulskis at the Broad,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.802
who's going to talk
about-- they are, I'll say,

00:07:12.802 --> 00:07:15.010
one of the largest-- I'll
let Kris decide if he wants

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.390
to say "the largest" or not.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:20.050
But they are one of the
largest genomic data factories

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:23.220
in the world that actually
does that primary analysis.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.120
Therefore, they have a large
factory data analysis need.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.570
And you'll hear him
talking about that.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.530
Ilya Shmulevich
from ISB, Institute

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.100
for Systems Biology
in Seattle, is

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:34.600
going to talk about
the work they're

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:38.860
doing, moving downstream to
actually explore that cancer

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.690
cloud data in the cloud.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.680
Igor Bogicevic
from Seven Bridges

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.680
is going to talk
about an application

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.410
that they have built that does
genomic analysis in the cloud.

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:51.560
They've had that application
running on Amazon for awhile,

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:53.810
and they've recently made
it available on Google Cloud

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:54.320
Platform.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.950
He's going to talk to you about
his experience doing that.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:58.940
And then intentionally,
we're wrapping up

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:00.840
with Jason Mercker
from Stanford,

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:02.890
who's actually the
reason all of us

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:04.490
are doing all the rest of this.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.360
He works at making
people healthier.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.290
And he's going to
talk about how we

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:10.720
can take all of this
other work, and what

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.950
does the tip of the
spear look like when

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:16.420
we try to put it into practice.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:19.200
So with that, I'm going
to hand it off to Kris

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.614
to talk about the factory.

00:08:21.614 --> 00:08:25.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:26.916 --> 00:08:28.287
KRIS CIBULSKIS: All right, hi.

00:08:28.287 --> 00:08:29.370
Hi, so I'm Kris Cibulskis.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:30.821
I'm from the Broad Institute.

00:08:30.821 --> 00:08:33.320
I'm here to talk about our first
year in the cloud of Google

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.218
Cloud Platform.

00:08:35.218 --> 00:08:37.634
So briefly, you may not have
heard of the Broad Institute.

00:08:37.634 --> 00:08:40.220
So let me tell you just a
little bit about who we are.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.262
We're a nonprofit
research institute.

00:08:42.262 --> 00:08:43.720
We were founded
with a $100 million

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:48.300
gift from Eli and Edythe Broad
back in 2003 as an experiment,

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.920
really, in a different way
to try to transform medicine.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.430
We're currently powered
by about 2,400 "Broadies."

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:56.070
Our stated approach
to do this is

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.590
to enable researchers to act
nimbly, to work boldly, share

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.692
openly, and reach globally.

00:09:02.692 --> 00:09:04.400
And as David mentioned,
since our leading

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.090
role in the sequencing of
the first human genome,

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:09.610
we've been a world leader in
the generation and analysis

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:10.660
of genomic data.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:12.680
And in this case, I
think we are the largest

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.400
producer of genomic data,
the largest single producer.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:21.670
We produce upwards of 10% of
the world's genomic data output.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.220
But with that comes
some challenges.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.530
So we struggle with
compute and storage.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:29.140
We have 13,000 cores
on prem, but analyzing

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:30.890
a single one of these
human genome samples

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:32.522
can take eight to 10 days.

00:09:32.522 --> 00:09:34.730
And doing an exercise where
you look across thousands

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.200
or tens of thousands of
samples to look at variation

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.360
can take upwards of 90 days.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.840
We've got 10 petabytes
of genomic data in-house.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.809
We're adding half
a petabyte a month.

00:09:43.809 --> 00:09:45.850
And we asked ourselves,
can we leverage the cloud

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:47.141
to help address these problems?

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.820
So let's talk about our
genomic data factory.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:54.920
Just for a sense of
scale, as David mentioned,

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.810
one human genome currently
requires about 100 gigabytes

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.460
of storage and takes
roughly 70 core-days

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:04.960
to complete analysis,
QC, and detect variation.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.610
Our sequencer fleet can produce
140 genomes a day currently.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.680
That's 14 terabytes
of storage every day,

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:13.320
and 10,000 core-days
of compute every day,

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.120
just to keep up
with the pipeline.

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:19.350
Oh, and we can scale that by
20% in just to three weeks.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.170
This happened back
in December, and it

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.100
could happen again at any time.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:25.480
So it's really this increase
in sequencing capability

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.531
that drives our compute
and storage needs.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:32.490
So how do we leverage
the elasticity, cost,

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.580
and reliability of the
Google Cloud Platform

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:35.855
for our genomic data factory?

00:10:35.855 --> 00:10:37.530
And this isn't a
one-time experiment.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.280
It's something that
has to run 24 by 7.

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.180
We can't rewrite all of the
tools to be cloud-native.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.660
They're not under our
control, not all of them.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:48.750
And parallel implementations
is undesirable.

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:51.490
And finally, we'd like our
computational biologists,

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:53.380
our scientists--
not the software

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.420
engineers-- to be able to read
and write these pipelines.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.740
The pipelines evolve
quickly, and we want

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.940
them to be able to keep pace.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.850
So to enable this
transition to the cloud,

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.100
we developed an open
source software stack along

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:09.100
with Google Genomics, starting
with a workflow description

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:12.100
language called WDL, which
is an external DSL used

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:13.850
by the computational
biologists in order

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:16.540
to express their
analytical pipelines.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.760
This is handed off to an
execution service called

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:20.510
Cromwell-- and both
these are open source,

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:23.480
and the GitHub
URLs are up there.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:27.430
This is an execution service
for WDL with multiple pluggable

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:30.950
backends for executing against
local docker-based backends,

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:35.190
grid engine, and finally also,
using the Google Genomics API,

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:37.010
Pipeline's API,
which we co-developed

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:38.340
with Google Genomics.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:40.340
And you could think of
this as a scalable doctor

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:43.550
as a service with
data scheduling.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:47.510
So with this, we began to port
our data factory to the cloud.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:49.270
And here's the
results of that port.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:50.540
It's highly scalable.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.400
We can currently run
100 genomes a day.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.191
We actually just finished
a run even bigger

00:11:54.191 --> 00:11:55.565
than that just this morning.

00:11:55.565 --> 00:11:58.190
We have dramatic improvement in
our turnaround time per genome.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:11:59.690
We've gone from the
eight to 10 days

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:02.890
I mentioned before for a
sample down to under 24 hours.

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:04.600
And we've got a
huge cost savings.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.220
From the very first run we
did six months ago on GCP,

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:09.120
we've reduced cost by over 80%.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.657
This is by taking advantage
of good technologies,

00:12:11.657 --> 00:12:13.990
by splitting apart our tasks
into machines that are just

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:16.790
the right size and only
paying for the CPUs and memory

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.522
that each task is using.

00:12:18.522 --> 00:12:21.300
Using preemptible VMs
is another huge win.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:26.020
Going down to cheaper disks when
the particular steps were not

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:27.150
I/O dependent.

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:29.090
And finally, using
smarter zone selections

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:31.067
that were not preempted
as often as we

00:12:31.067 --> 00:12:32.400
would be with a naive selection.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.680
And also, as part of our
mission to reach globally,

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:39.210
this exact same
pipeline is being

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:41.544
delivered with Google Genomics
to the rest of the world.

00:12:41.544 --> 00:12:42.960
This is the
pipeline-as-a-service.

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.622
It's available with a simple
API call at the URL listed here,

00:12:45.622 --> 00:12:46.580
and it's available now.

00:12:49.214 --> 00:12:51.130
So now that we've got
our genomic data factory

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:53.296
in the cloud, we wanted to
turn back to our problems

00:12:53.296 --> 00:12:55.320
with genomic data storage.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.020
As I mentioned before, we're
running out of storage on prem.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:12:59.560
We've got 10
petabytes of storage,

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:01.660
and we're growing half
a petabyte a month.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:03.560
A lot of this data is
infrequently accessed,

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:05.410
and we needed a
scalable archive.

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.260
And in this case, we looked
to a technology called Avere.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.070
And Avere sits between
the Google Cloud Storage

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.170
over our internet
[INAUDIBLE] 10 gig connection

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.830
and presents an NFS mount point
to the rest of our cluster.

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:18.030
So we can move data
onto a mount point.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:20.720
It's transparently compressed,
encrypted, and transmitted

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:21.749
to Google Cloud.

00:13:21.749 --> 00:13:23.790
And we've transferred over
two petabytes this way

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:25.248
this month, offloading
the pressure

00:13:25.248 --> 00:13:26.288
for our on-prem storage.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.893
Now, with elastic storage, we
also would like cheap storage.

00:13:31.893 --> 00:13:34.510
10 petabytes is a
lot of data to store.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:36.850
And genomic data has some
interesting properties.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:39.240
Very small subsets of that
100-gigabyte band file

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.850
are accessed frequently,
hundreds or thousands of times.

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:44.120
But it's on the order of
megabytes or kilobytes.

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:46.530
And the full data set is
accessed maybe once or twice

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:47.620
per year.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:49.510
And in this case,
GCS Nearline appears

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:52.570
to be a near-perfect storage
temperature for this problem.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:54.760
It's cheaper, about
$0.01 per gig per month,

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:56.811
as opposed to $0.026
cents for online.

00:13:56.811 --> 00:13:58.810
There is a data retrieval
cost, but again, we're

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:01.300
not retrieving all
our data that often.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:02.970
And we've got high
I/O throughput,

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.150
because your I/O
throughput is basically

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:07.500
enabled by the amount of total
data that you're storing.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:09.416
And so with 10 petabytes
or more being stored,

00:14:09.416 --> 00:14:10.730
we get high throughput.

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:12.620
And we can save over
$1.5 million a year

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:14.435
currently just with
these access patterns.

00:14:17.067 --> 00:14:18.775
OK, so we've got our
factory in the cloud

00:14:18.775 --> 00:14:21.984
and we've got our storage
problem under control.

00:14:21.984 --> 00:14:23.900
Now we want to enable
this downstream analysis

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.160
that David talked about.

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:29.580
Taking a brief step back
about how large-scale genomics

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:32.040
research is accomplished,
the traditional way

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.400
is to bring the data
to the researchers.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.120
So the TCGA project that
David mentioned before, we

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:38.740
generated a petabyte of data.

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:41.050
It was put into a
large national archive.

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:42.760
And step one if you
want to use this data

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:46.240
is download a petabyte of data
to your local institute, right?

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:46.990
This is terrible.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:49.750
Data sharing is the same
as data copying now.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:51.209
This requires big
infrastructure.

00:14:51.209 --> 00:14:53.000
You've got to have
enough compute, storage,

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:54.184
and network connectivity.

00:14:54.184 --> 00:14:56.100
It shuts out a ton of
people with great ideas,

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:58.460
because they simply don't have
that kind of infrastructure.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:14:59.380
Of course, it's fixed.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.220
It's not elastic.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:02.860
And also, the security
implementations

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:04.360
at each one of these
different sites

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.160
that download this
protected data

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:07.530
is different and inconsistent.

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.030
They may be good,
some might be bad,

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:10.995
but they're certainly
not consistent.

00:15:10.995 --> 00:15:12.370
So the cloud way,
right, would be

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:14.080
to bring the
researchers to the data.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:15.640
We've got all the
data in the cloud.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.750
We've got elastic
compute around it.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:20.100
Data sharing is
truly data sharing.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:22.230
We've got, as I mentioned,
elastic compute.

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.540
And finally, we've got
a centralized security

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:26.700
implementation,
so we can be sure

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:29.250
that it's a high quality
implementation and consistent.

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.870
So with these challenges in mind
and taking part in the National

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:36.660
Cancer Institute's
Cloud Pilot Project,

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:39.170
we developed a system
called FireCloud.

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:40.660
And what is FireCloud?

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:42.860
It's an open-source
platform with APIs

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:45.220
and a web portal for
securely managing, sharing,

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:47.824
and analyzing genomics
data in the cloud.

00:15:47.824 --> 00:15:49.990
And it's also the way that
our genomics data factory

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:51.448
is going to deliver
all of our data

00:15:51.448 --> 00:15:55.120
that we produce to
the researchers.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:56.680
How did we build this?

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:58.740
We built it on top of
core Google Cloud Platform

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.190
technologies-- for rapid
development for one thing,

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:04.570
but also we wanted security
and reliability and scalability

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:06.194
that GCP brings.

00:16:06.194 --> 00:16:08.110
You can see in this image
here, the blue boxes

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:09.390
are things that we've
custom developed.

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:11.780
In the center here is the
workflow description language

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:14.270
and Cromwell execution service
that powers our own genomics

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:15.390
data factory.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.350
But it sits on top of the
Google Genomics pipeline API

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:20.780
compute engine
Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.250
And we also use Google
Identities for access control

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:24.370
to all of our data.

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:27.600
So when users log into the
system and access their data,

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:30.320
they can also go to GCP
directly and see their same data

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:32.222
in their own buckets
and enhance and extend

00:16:32.222 --> 00:16:33.180
the system in that way.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:34.721
You're not locked
in to the platform.

00:16:34.721 --> 00:16:36.920
The data is yours.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:38.330
The system is in
alpha right now,

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.496
but we're launching in early
April at FireCloud.org.

00:16:44.516 --> 00:16:45.890
So I could've
stopped here, but I

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:47.285
asked if I could talk a
little bit about, well,

00:16:47.285 --> 00:16:48.451
what could have gone better?

00:16:48.451 --> 00:16:50.704
Not everything was 100% perfect.

00:16:50.704 --> 00:16:52.620
The first thing was that
the security controls

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:55.780
as we started our project
were fairly coarse-grained,

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:58.464
although the talk right
before this was about the IAM

00:16:58.464 --> 00:17:00.130
features that Google
is now rolling out.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:01.630
And these are really
exciting to us,

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:03.640
and we're going to keep
a close eye on them.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:05.139
The second problem
that we found was

00:17:05.139 --> 00:17:08.089
with GCS buckets, if a data
owner gives read access

00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:10.349
to other people,
they're responsible for

00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:11.631
any egress charges incurred.

00:17:11.631 --> 00:17:13.589
So we'd like you to keep
the data in the cloud,

00:17:13.589 --> 00:17:16.140
but we can't prevent you from
taking it out of the cloud.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.270
And this encourages the
anti-pattern of data

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:20.219
copying, rather than
sharing in place.

00:17:23.349 --> 00:17:25.900
So finally, what's
next for us in 2016?

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:27.899
We're going to complete
a full transition to GCP

00:17:27.899 --> 00:17:29.834
for our genomics data factory.

00:17:29.834 --> 00:17:31.500
We're going to publicly
launch FireCloud

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:32.417
in the next few weeks.

00:17:32.417 --> 00:17:34.416
We're going to try to
continue to reduce storage

00:17:34.416 --> 00:17:36.690
costs by using GCS Nearline
and other advanced data

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:37.940
compression.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:40.600
And we're going to enhance our
analytic methods-- the GATK

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:42.810
in particular, that's part
of this primary analysis

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:44.310
pipeline-- to take
advantage of more

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:46.101
cloud-friendly
technologies, such as spark,

00:17:46.101 --> 00:17:47.800
data proc, and BigQuery.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:48.570
And we need help.

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:52.080
So if you're interested,
please come seek us out.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:53.737
All right, thanks
for your attention.

00:17:53.737 --> 00:17:56.898
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:57.762 --> 00:17:59.650
And I'll turn it
over to Ilya now.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.430
ILYA SHMULEVICH:
Hello, everyone.

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:09.790
My name is Ilya Shmulevich.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:12.770
I am from the Institute
for Systems Biology

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:14.880
in Seattle, Washington.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.430
And I would like to tell you
about our effort, the ISB

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.070
Cancer Genomics Cloud.

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:21.360
First, I would like to
acknowledge our team

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:24.080
at the ISB, with
our project lead

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:27.820
Sheila Reynolds and our
outstanding partners in Google

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:31.940
Genomics, particular Jonathan
Bingham and his team,

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:37.370
as well as CSRA and
David Pot and his team.

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:40.360
We have been part of the
Cancer Genome Atlas Project

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:41.990
for about six years.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:44.550
David has already
introduced this project.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:48.840
It has generated very large
amounts of comprehensive cancer

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:54.040
genomic data, unprecedented
in its size and scope.

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:58.860
And as part of the cancer
genome atlas as a data analysis

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:02.700
center, we have been
building data integration

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:07.950
and analytical tools, as well
as interactive visualization

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:10.500
and exploration
tools, to put back

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:14.380
into the hands of the
cancer research community.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:16.240
The great challenge
has been that we

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:20.320
have had to precompute a lot of
that analysis-- so for example,

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:23.690
run correlations among the
different types of data

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:27.040
collected in the project and
then offer the results back

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:28.950
to the researchers.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:33.380
Of course, that is problematic
because every researcher has

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:35.200
a different question
in mind, and they

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:38.610
want to ask different types
of questions of the data.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:41.740
So the challenge is
really to, as Kris just

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:45.570
said, bring the
compute to the data,

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:47.930
bring the researcher
to the data.

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:52.670
And this is indeed what we have
embarked on with the ISB-CGC,

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:56.040
and that is to make the TCGA
data available, together

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.510
with the tools and
the compute power,

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:03.960
to a broad range of users
through various access modes.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:08.710
And this includes interactive
web applications; scripting

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:13.050
languages, such as R
and Python and SQL;

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:17.240
as well as direct
programmatic access.

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:19.770
So these different
modes of access

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:23.080
generally correspond
to these three persona

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:25.420
that we have shown here.

00:20:25.420 --> 00:20:28.950
The PI biologist, the
computational research

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:32.310
scientist, and the
algorithm developer.

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:37.140
So the PI slash biologist
may want to access these data

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:39.500
and explore them
in various ways,

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:42.850
look at correlations and
statistical associations,

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.370
ask different types of
questions of the data

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:48.260
in an interactive way,
but without having to do

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:51.120
any programming or scripting.

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:54.270
And the computational
research scientist

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:58.030
may want to combine different
types of analyses that are not

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:01.980
available through a web
application-- may want to bring

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:05.510
an additional datasets,
their own data sets,

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:09.010
in order to compare
them with the TCGA data.

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:12.990
And the algorithm developer
may wish to actually develop

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:17.340
a new algorithm that performs
some fundamental analysis

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:21.390
on the data, such as identifying
the mutations in the cancer

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:22.900
genomes.

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:26.030
And obviously, all of
these different users

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:28.050
would like to access
the same data,

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:30.390
but in very different ways.

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:34.880
And our platform is built
on a lot of powerful Google

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:38.430
technologies, such as
cloud storage for storing

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:42.880
the files the object store
for all of the TCGA data;

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:49.010
BigQuery, for storing all
of the heterogeneous data

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:53.990
and accessing it through a very
powerful analytical engine that

00:21:53.990 --> 00:21:57.760
allows you to aggregate
results from enormous datasets

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:01.120
literally in seconds; and
through the Google Genomics

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:04.420
API, which is a platform
that is optimized

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:08.340
for storing sequence reads
that come off the sequencer.

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:10.770
And it can hand handle
petabytes of data.

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:17.660
So we have already placed all
of the TCGA data, approximately

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:20.480
one petabyte of it,
onto Google Cloud

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.780
Platform that is
stored on Google Cloud

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:25.380
Storage and BigQuery.

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:29.490
And most of these data
are sequencing data,

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:32.760
if you count in terms of size.

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:38.030
So 99.8% of the data
are sequence data, DNA

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:40.740
and some RNA sequence data.

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.360
However, if you look
at the number of files

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:48.110
and the heterogeneity and
the complexity of the data,

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:51.840
about 0.2% of the
data in terms of size

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:56.250
actually comprise about
340,000 different files

00:22:56.250 --> 00:23:00.780
that contain clinical data
and protein expression data,

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:04.020
and data on different
parts of the genome that

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:07.380
are amplified or deleted,
and a lot of other types

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:09.070
of measurement data.

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:12.420
So this is really a great
challenge for researchers.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:14.420
It's not just the
size of the data,

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:16.240
but it's really the complexity.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.740
How do we put it all
together and analyze it

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:21.600
in an integrative fashion?

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:25.330
So we have loaded all of
these datasets into BigQuery.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:29.780
So every type of data is
available in its own BigQuery

00:23:29.780 --> 00:23:33.270
table that then
allows us to integrate

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:36.480
them to query these
different BigQuery tables

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:42.220
and integrate the
results to perform more

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:46.250
comprehensive analysis than is
possible with individual data

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:48.710
types alone.

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:52.560
So let's go through these
three access modes quickly.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.270
So the PI slash
biologist would like

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:57.830
to access different
types of cancer data sets

00:23:57.830 --> 00:24:03.240
and look at various
associations in the data.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:06.420
So you could, for
instance, construct

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:10.090
a cohort that is comprised
of multiple different cancer

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:10.620
types.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:14.800
There are 33 different cancer
types available in TCGA.

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:17.510
So suppose you wanted
to study the cancers

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:22.180
of the digestive tract by
combining esophageal, stomach

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:25.960
cancer, and colon cancer
together in one dataset,

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:29.120
and then analyzing that dataset
and perhaps comparing it

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:30.310
with another one.

00:24:30.310 --> 00:24:33.560
Or you could construct
a cohort that is based

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:35.420
on some molecular feature.

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:39.090
For example, you could look
at all 33 counts or types

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:41.520
and select only
the ones that have

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:45.530
a mutation in a particular
gene of interest.

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:46.180
OK.

00:24:46.180 --> 00:24:49.360
And then we have
various visualizations

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:52.600
that are wired to
these BigQuery tables

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:56.920
that interactively allow the
user to explore the data.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:59.550
For example, we have been
working with the Integrated

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:02.990
Genome Viewer Team
from UCSD, and have

00:25:02.990 --> 00:25:07.810
deployed the IGB viewer that
can fetch the data directly

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:10.230
from the Google Genomics API.

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:13.920
So it can get the reads and
display them graphically

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.480
in an interactive fashion.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:19.290
So if you're looking for that
mutation in the cancer genome,

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:21.740
you can then see the
evidence for that mutation

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:23.000
in those reads.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.120
We also have scatter
plots that can

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:28.300
get the data from
different BigQuery tables

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:32.730
and display them interactively
in a scatter plot like this,

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:38.340
where you can color different
samples by the cancer types.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:41.500
Or, in the far right,
you see a visualization

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:43.670
of mutation frequencies.

00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:46.110
We're looking at a
particular protein

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:48.810
and across many different
types of cancer,

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:52.280
and we can see the places
on the protein that

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.130
harbor these mutations.

00:25:54.130 --> 00:25:55.830
You can already
visually see that there

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:58.890
are certain hotspots where
these mutations occur

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:03.020
more frequently than in
other places in the genome.

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:04.890
If you're a
computational scientist,

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:06.560
you probably want
to write scripts.

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:12.550
You want to analyze the data
with R or with Python or SQL.

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:16.750
So we have created a number
of examples and templates

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:19.420
to make it easier for
you to get started.

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:23.420
For each of these data types
that is available in BigQuery,

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:26.560
we have templates written
in R and in Python

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:29.070
that show you how you
can fetch the data

00:26:29.070 --> 00:26:31.690
and then do something
simple with them.

00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:34.120
If you like to use
packages, such as

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:37.880
the popular Bioconductor
suite of open-source software

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:40.150
for bioinformatics,
you could do that.

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:43.690
Or if you'd like to query the
Google Genomics API directly

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:48.500
from your R client, you
could do that as well.

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:51.320
So here is one quick
example in Python,

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:54.550
actually running on
Google Cloud Datalab, that

00:26:54.550 --> 00:26:59.120
fetches so-called copy number
variation data from BigQuery.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:03.370
This data specifies
which parts of the genome

00:27:03.370 --> 00:27:06.010
have more copies
and less copies.

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:08.460
You may remember from
your high school biology

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:11.520
that we all have two copies
of every part of our genome,

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:13.840
one from our mom and
one from our dad.

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:17.090
But in cancer cells, very
often there are more copies.

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:20.220
You could have three or
four or five or more copies.

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:22.290
And also, you may
have less copies.

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:26.090
You might have only one copy of
your gene, or even zero copies

00:27:26.090 --> 00:27:28.600
when the gene is
completely deleted.

00:27:28.600 --> 00:27:32.430
So we can look at these
data across 11,000 tumors,

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:35.310
look at millions of these
segments of the genome

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:38.230
to see whether they are
amplified or deleted,

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:42.510
and draw a histogram like
this in roughly two seconds,

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:46.080
just by fetching all of this
information from BigQuery

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:48.650
and displaying it
as a histogram.

00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:51.310
This is really
transformational for research,

00:27:51.310 --> 00:27:54.270
because it opens
up possibilities

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:57.780
of analyzing very large
data sets quickly, getting

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:00.270
the answers, and using
those answers to ask

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:01.410
the next question.

00:28:01.410 --> 00:28:05.710
Because research is really
an iterative process.

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:07.145
If you're an
algorithm developer,

00:28:07.145 --> 00:28:09.140
you own your own Google project.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:12.710
You get automatic access
to Google's Cloud Storage,

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:16.530
BigQuery, the Google
Genomics API, and as well

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.990
as the other Google
Compute technologies,

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:23.620
such as Compute Engine,
Container Engine, and Dataflow.

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:26.960
Of course, you can invite other
people to your collaborators,

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:30.810
to your Google Cloud project.

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.710
We also use the
Cloud Endpoints API,

00:28:33.710 --> 00:28:37.470
which is backed by App Engine
so that you can do everything

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:38.900
from the command
line that you can

00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:40.940
do from the web application.

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:42.600
For example, you
can authenticate

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:44.070
from the command line.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:47.460
You can make requests to
get a list of your cohorts

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:53.070
or look at the details of your
cohort, save a new cohort,

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:57.570
or get a list of data files that
is associated with a cohort.

00:28:57.570 --> 00:29:01.030
I would like to end by
acknowledging the National

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:05.120
Cancer Institute for
funding these projects,

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:09.100
and invite you to
visit the ISB CGC.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:09.600
Thank you.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:14.370
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:21.340
IGOR BOGICEVIC: Hi, everybody.

00:29:21.340 --> 00:29:24.516
I'm Igor Bogicevic
from Seven Bridges.

00:29:24.516 --> 00:29:27.530
And first of all, before
I get into how it actually

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:31.950
looked like for us to move
from AWS to Google Cloud

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:35.640
and what were the challenges and
actual results of this process,

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:38.380
I would like to tell you a
little bit about what we do.

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:42.290
We are building the robust,
scalable, and secure platform

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:46.670
for actually data analysis
and biomedical analysis,

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:50.710
with a specific emphasis
on the genetic data.

00:29:50.710 --> 00:29:54.270
And to do that, we
actually had to build

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:58.210
various aspects of the platform
that are completely detached

00:29:58.210 --> 00:30:01.160
from the actual infrastructure.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:04.840
And we are also the strong
supporters of open standards

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:06.480
and reproducibility.

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:08.280
We support the common
worker language,

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:13.366
and we are one of the leading
members of the community push

00:30:13.366 --> 00:30:16.080
to CWL to actually
bring competition

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:19.030
from your workstation
to your cluster,

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:20.770
and eventually to cloud.

00:30:20.770 --> 00:30:25.470
And we designed our platform
for portability, which basically

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:30.560
means that our primary goal was
to build a platform in a way

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:33.610
that you can actually bring
the platform to the data,

00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:36.360
and wherever this
data sit, it doesn't

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:42.340
mind for us whether your data is
in AWS, Google, or your phone.

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:44.950
And if your phone would be a
good computational resource,

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:46.700
there is no reason why
we wouldn't use it.

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:48.660
But we are not there quite yet.

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:52.460
So we actually rely
on the cloud platforms

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:56.200
as such to actually bring
the computational analysis

00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:57.240
to the end users.

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:59.920
And we've found that the
Google Cloud was fantastic

00:30:59.920 --> 00:31:01.550
infrastructure for us.

00:31:01.550 --> 00:31:05.490
And [INAUDIBLE] how we
look under the hood.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:08.990
This is a small diagram
that very generally shows

00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:12.560
how we actually decoupled
our infrastructure

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:15.260
from the actual resources.

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:17.320
And this helped us a
great deal to actually get

00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:18.600
to the Google Cloud.

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:21.190
Of course, it's not easy to
move from one infrastructure

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:23.320
to the other infrastructure
and actually build

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:24.630
the whole platform.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:27.710
But given the great resources we
could find in the Google Cloud,

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:30.980
we found it to be
even easier for us

00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:32.680
than we actually
anticipated originally

00:31:32.680 --> 00:31:35.350
to support Google
Cloud Platform as well.

00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:37.750
So why are we building all this?

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:41.950
So why would anyone want
to build this platform?

00:31:41.950 --> 00:31:48.260
As you heard, we are working in
a space that's very exciting.

00:31:48.260 --> 00:31:51.580
And there is a huge promise
that all the work we are doing

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:54.310
will actually give the
benefits to the end users.

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:57.770
And our customers are
working on genomics research

00:31:57.770 --> 00:32:00.910
for cancer drug development
and precision medicine.

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:02.550
And a couple of
projects we're really

00:32:02.550 --> 00:32:07.080
proud of that we worked on
is the Pan-Cancer Analysis

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:07.750
of Whole Genome.

00:32:07.750 --> 00:32:10.535
And we helped the International
Cancer Genome Consortium

00:32:10.535 --> 00:32:12.260
to actually finish
the whole process

00:32:12.260 --> 00:32:17.640
and process over 500 cancer
genomes in less than two days.

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:22.300
We are also one of the NCI
Cancer Genomics Cloud pilots.

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:24.290
And we are happy to
share this stage together

00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:25.860
with the other two pilots.

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:29.530
And we are only industry
trusted partner to do so.

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:32.760
Moreover, we also
are working together

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:36.170
with Genomics England
and 100k Genomes Project.

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:37.820
And we are actually
to them trying

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:43.580
to commercialize our
[INAUDIBLE] and solutions

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:47.082
for actually
populational genomics

00:32:47.082 --> 00:32:49.720
through the graph genome.

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:52.360
And moreover, we are also
very happy to announce

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:55.040
two projects that
are directly related

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:57.730
to President Obama's
Personalized Medicine

00:32:57.730 --> 00:32:58.230
Initiative.

00:32:58.230 --> 00:33:01.390
First one is Cavatica, that
we're building together

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:05.100
with Children's Hospital
of Philadelphia.

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:08.480
It's actually trying to
address children cancer.

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:12.730
And we also have recently signed
a credo together with Lockheed

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:14.390
Martin, and we are
working together

00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:17.800
with Veterans Affairs on
Million Veteran Project data.

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:20.090
And we're trying to help
them analyze currently

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:22.750
the biggest cohort
of [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:25.430
typical and atypical
paired data.

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:29.987
So let's get to what we are
really here in our story.

00:33:29.987 --> 00:33:31.450
So I'm going to
share a short war

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:35.190
story of how this whole part
of our platform will look like.

00:33:35.190 --> 00:33:37.820
And it's actually quite
boring, which is good.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:40.300
Boring in software is
usually very, very good.

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:44.900
So what we actually
found is that we

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:47.800
had relatively compatible
computational and storage

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:50.740
resources, which
means it was not

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:53.990
very hard for us to transfer
our whole platform to the Google

00:33:53.990 --> 00:33:55.160
Cloud.

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:57.510
And we didn't have
any major blockers.

00:33:57.510 --> 00:33:59.560
Actually, the major
challenges were

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:02.580
how could we actually
optimally use

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:06.770
the computational resources,
and how to actually benchmark

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:09.810
those resources and figure
out how to use optimally

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:13.630
the whole infrastructure
that Google provided for us--

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:16.840
and especially, how to figure
out the efficient transfer

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:18.159
to and from Google Storage.

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:20.000
Because as you
heard, we are working

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:23.040
with very, very large data
sets, and speed of transfer

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:24.440
really matters.

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:27.420
And what we really liked
is that we actually could

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:29.590
design custom machine types.

00:34:29.590 --> 00:34:31.110
And that means that
you can actually

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:35.159
design the machine type that
fits the computation and tools

00:34:35.159 --> 00:34:38.429
you're running the best.

00:34:38.429 --> 00:34:42.659
You can actually choose how
many CPU power do you need,

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:43.830
how many memory you need.

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:45.550
And actually, you
can also attach

00:34:45.550 --> 00:34:47.830
the very, very
flexible and fast SSD

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:49.790
storage, which really mattered.

00:34:49.790 --> 00:34:53.909
And one thing, we also found
that it was cheaper on average

00:34:53.909 --> 00:34:57.180
and much simpler to manage
computational resources

00:34:57.180 --> 00:35:01.420
for various compliances compared
to AWS, where we had to deal

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:02.940
with dedicated tendency.

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:05.770
Here, everything that we used
was pretty much HIPAA-compliant

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:08.720
and could be designed for
other compliances, as well.

00:35:08.720 --> 00:35:12.500
So I would like to share the
results of our benchmarks.

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:16.600
You can see relatively
similar instance types here.

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:20.265
What we found very exciting,
besides just the much faster

00:35:20.265 --> 00:35:24.820
average instance book, is that
the total cost is 40% lower.

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:28.360
And that's even before using
preemptable virtual machines,

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:32.670
which significantly
reduce costs even further.

00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:35.277
And just to give an
overview of the benchmark,

00:35:35.277 --> 00:35:37.430
we used so-called Seven
Bridges whole genome

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:38.780
sequencing pipeline.

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:43.360
And we used Illumina test read
files and standard references.

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:46.520
And we already
mentioned CWL pipeline,

00:35:46.520 --> 00:35:48.970
which you can actually
easily transfer it

00:35:48.970 --> 00:35:54.430
from CGC Seven Bridges platform
to Amazon Seven Bridges

00:35:54.430 --> 00:35:56.870
platform to Google
Seven Bridges platform.

00:35:56.870 --> 00:36:01.100
And one thing that actually
makes this benchmark

00:36:01.100 --> 00:36:05.050
not so super exact is that
the instance types are not

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:07.624
super similar.

00:36:07.624 --> 00:36:11.420
They're as similar as possible,
but not exactly the same.

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:15.530
However, this was the machine
that you've seen here mentioned

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:18.270
is actually the machine
type that was pretty much

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:21.490
benchmarked as the fastest
instance type on Amazon

00:36:21.490 --> 00:36:23.030
platform that we could find.

00:36:23.030 --> 00:36:28.130
So besides that, I could say
that this platform is alive

00:36:28.130 --> 00:36:30.910
currently on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:36:30.910 --> 00:36:33.270
And you can access
it through the URL.

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:34.410
You can create the account.

00:36:34.410 --> 00:36:37.030
You can play with the pipeline
so you have some free credit.

00:36:37.030 --> 00:36:38.020
You can check it out.

00:36:38.020 --> 00:36:43.820
If you like it, you
can run [INAUDIBLE],

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:45.360
use it with already
existing data.

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:47.730
And we will encourage
you to do so.

00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:51.730
And just to conclude, so
our conclusion basically

00:36:51.730 --> 00:36:53.630
is why we find
this important, why

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:59.420
we find the significantly
lower amounts of money

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:03.570
that we spend on executing
pipelines is important.

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:07.040
Any dollar that we actually
don't spend on the compute

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:09.820
is actually a dollar that can
be used to generate the data,

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:12.700
further analyze data, and
actually get to the point

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:14.440
why we are actually
even building this.

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:18.580
And this is creating the better
treatments for the patients,

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:22.900
and actually further improve
on the personalized medicine

00:37:22.900 --> 00:37:24.998
and health in general.

00:37:24.998 --> 00:37:26.492
Thank you.

00:37:26.492 --> 00:37:29.480
[APPLAUSE]

