requirements for cloud compute

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:14.820
and storage.

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:18.530
And I'll touch on those briefly.

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:22.820
So we started as a pilot
in January of 2014,

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:26.640
where we developed the workflow
and evaluated the feasibility

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:29.050
by working through 100 cases.

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:32.680
We use genome sequencing to
evaluate the cause of disease

00:38:32.680 --> 00:38:36.060
in patients with suspected
genetic disorders.

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:39.760
This involves looking at
2.85 billion positions

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:42.150
throughout an
individual's genome.

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:44.470
So we focus on four
disease areas--

00:38:44.470 --> 00:38:47.880
childhood mystery
diseases, familial cancer,

00:38:47.880 --> 00:38:50.490
familial heart disease,
and unusual reactions

00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:53.010
to medications.

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:55.320
However, I think the best
way to describe what we do

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:56.836
is to walk you through a case.

00:38:56.836 --> 00:38:58.210
This is a family
that's been seen

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:00.760
in our medical center
for over 10 years,

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:03.820
and despite very extensive
testing as well as

00:39:03.820 --> 00:39:06.320
consultations from a
variety of physicians,

00:39:06.320 --> 00:39:09.600
the diagnosis remained unknown.

00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:13.220
So this falls into the general
category of pediatric mystery

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:14.310
diseases.

00:39:14.310 --> 00:39:16.300
And this was two
siblings, both who had

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:18.770
very similar clinical findings.

00:39:18.770 --> 00:39:22.510
So they presented early in life
with progressive cerebellar

00:39:22.510 --> 00:39:23.890
ataxia.

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:27.370
This is a lack of control
during voluntary movements,

00:39:27.370 --> 00:39:30.410
such as walking or
picking up objects.

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:32.650
This is associated with
atrophy or shrinkage

00:39:32.650 --> 00:39:35.220
of the back portion of the
brain, the cerebellum, that

00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:37.270
controls such movements.

00:39:37.270 --> 00:39:40.730
In addition, the patients
exhibit intellectual disability

00:39:40.730 --> 00:39:44.940
and the other findings
that I've described here.

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:48.130
So we performed genome
sequencing on the family

00:39:48.130 --> 00:39:52.870
and identified two
inactive copies of SNX14.

00:39:52.870 --> 00:39:55.690
And although this
finding was suspicious,

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:58.310
it was insufficient
to act on clinically

00:39:58.310 --> 00:40:01.040
because the gene had not
been previously implicated

00:40:01.040 --> 00:40:04.020
in human disease.

00:40:04.020 --> 00:40:07.440
Through an essentially
random hallway chat,

00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:09.710
we learned of another
group that had actually

00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:14.530
observed 20 cases, all with
very similar clinical findings,

00:40:14.530 --> 00:40:18.100
and all of these 20 individuals
had two inactive copies,

00:40:18.100 --> 00:40:21.350
again of the SNX14 gene.

00:40:21.350 --> 00:40:23.700
So both copies were inactivated.

00:40:23.700 --> 00:40:26.210
So based on this, we had
sufficient information

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:29.010
to make a diagnosis.

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:30.940
And these findings were
eventually published.

00:40:30.940 --> 00:40:33.030
But if we were to wait
for the publication,

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:36.120
for the information to be
indexed and us to reanalyze

00:40:36.120 --> 00:40:38.710
the case, it may have taken
an additional two years

00:40:38.710 --> 00:40:40.310
to get to this point.

00:40:40.310 --> 00:40:42.110
So it really emphasizes
the importance

00:40:42.110 --> 00:40:44.419
of data-sharing efforts,
such as those that

00:40:44.419 --> 00:40:45.710
have been described here today.

00:40:48.280 --> 00:40:50.640
So what does this
mean for the family?

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:53.620
So it ends a 10-year
diagnostic odyssey.

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:55.400
It provides the
family information

00:40:55.400 --> 00:40:58.320
about the disease course
and associated management

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:02.610
approaches based on now a
cohort of 30 individuals

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:05.460
across at least 15 families.

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:07.750
The gene is involved
in a cellular process

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:11.410
for which targeted therapies
are under investigation.

00:41:11.410 --> 00:41:15.010
So there may be therapeutic
options in the future.

00:41:15.010 --> 00:41:17.460
However, it underscores
the importance

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:19.770
of arriving at a
diagnosis early,

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:24.190
before too much damage
is already done.

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:27.270
So the pilot was
deemed successful

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:29.260
by our supporting
organizations, and we

00:41:29.260 --> 00:41:33.230
were asked to transition from
a pilot to production phase.

00:41:33.230 --> 00:41:35.000
The pilot worked well.

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:38.640
However, it was slow
and really not scalable.

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:42.970
We need to be able go from
one patient to many patients.

00:41:42.970 --> 00:41:45.320
And there were
several challenges,

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:47.940
particularly involved in compute
and storage infrastructure,

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:51.020
which I'll describe today, which
we think Google Cloud provided

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:52.480
the answers.

00:41:52.480 --> 00:41:58.120
So beginning in the summer
of 2015, here are the issues.

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:00.630
So the first is
an issue of scale.

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:02.540
So conservatively,
we know that we

00:42:02.540 --> 00:42:05.510
need to examine about
3,000 patients per year

00:42:05.510 --> 00:42:07.170
in a health care system.

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:10.352
At the same time, we also
believe that genetic testing

00:42:10.352 --> 00:42:12.560
is going to be important
for the majority of patients

00:42:12.560 --> 00:42:13.900
in our health care system.

00:42:13.900 --> 00:42:15.250
But we don't know when.

00:42:15.250 --> 00:42:17.010
So we needed to
find a solution that

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:19.610
would allow us to scale
the need, including fairly

00:42:19.610 --> 00:42:21.440
rapid and massive scaling.

00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:24.660
An elastic cloud provides this.

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:27.090
So the second issue,
as I mentioned--

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:29.820
we are housed within
a clinical service,

00:42:29.820 --> 00:42:32.250
and there are three
entities involved with that.

00:42:32.250 --> 00:42:35.430
There are complex security,
legal, regulatory,

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:38.150
and other business requirements
associated with this.

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:40.570
And after due diligence,
these entities

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:43.550
felt comfortable with
appropriate configuration.

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:45.650
Google Cloud was an
appropriate place

00:42:45.650 --> 00:42:49.100
to put both protected
health information as well

00:42:49.100 --> 00:42:51.385
as genomic data.

00:42:51.385 --> 00:42:53.010
And then the last
thing we were looking

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:55.910
for is a team that had
additional experience

00:42:55.910 --> 00:42:59.860
in genetics and
genomics as a value add.

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:02.180
Stanford has many
ongoing collaborations

00:43:02.180 --> 00:43:04.880
with Google Genomics,
and we were confident

00:43:04.880 --> 00:43:07.650
that they would make
our service better.

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:10.640
In addition, thinking about
what we do on a day-to-day basis

00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:12.130
is essentially search.

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:14.910
We search the EMR for
certain clinical findings.

00:43:14.910 --> 00:43:18.400
We search the literature and
databases for variance genes,

00:43:18.400 --> 00:43:21.160
as well as associated
clinical findings.

00:43:21.160 --> 00:43:24.520
This is obviously a
core function of Google,

00:43:24.520 --> 00:43:26.340
and we think this
will help us provide

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:30.760
better genetics-guided
health care to our patients.

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:33.990
So the transition to
cloud is well underway.

00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:36.030
We're targeting a formal
launch of the service

00:43:36.030 --> 00:43:38.520
at the end of this year.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:40.020
And then we especially
want to thank

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:42.980
our patients and the
health care providers,

00:43:42.980 --> 00:43:45.460
likewise the many individuals
at Stanford Medicine,

00:43:45.460 --> 00:43:48.068
as well as Google, that
made this possible.

00:43:48.068 --> 00:43:49.067
Thank you for your time.

00:43:49.067 --> 00:43:51.989
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:56.380
DAVID GLAZER: All right.

00:43:56.380 --> 00:43:57.920
Thank you, Jason.

00:43:57.920 --> 00:44:00.440
A couple minutes of just
wrap-up-- what you've just seen

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:02.520
are several examples
of how people

00:44:02.520 --> 00:44:06.210
are building on top of
the cloud to move forward

00:44:06.210 --> 00:44:08.110
in the world of life science.

00:44:08.110 --> 00:44:10.830
The general theme here--
why does this all matter?

00:44:10.830 --> 00:44:13.600
Well, Jason ended
it with the example

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:15.040
of exactly why it matters.

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:16.580
It matters because
when one of us

00:44:16.580 --> 00:44:18.720
is sick, when someone
we love is sick,

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:21.140
we want to use the best
knowledge, the best

00:44:21.140 --> 00:44:24.170
information, bring it to
bear, the best understanding.

00:44:24.170 --> 00:44:27.020
The way we get that
knowledge is by studying

00:44:27.020 --> 00:44:28.640
thousands, and
hundreds of thousands,

00:44:28.640 --> 00:44:29.930
and millions of people.

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:32.700
That's where you need the scale
of data that's now possible.

00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:35.900
Wet science and dry
science have come together

00:44:35.900 --> 00:44:39.380
to really get this
virtuous cycle working.

00:44:39.380 --> 00:44:42.190
Two 10-second tastes
of where it's going--

00:44:42.190 --> 00:44:44.680
remember at the beginning,
I said a few years ago there

00:44:44.680 --> 00:44:47.070
was a very exciting
groundbreaking scale

00:44:47.070 --> 00:44:49.030
with the 1,000 genomes project.

00:44:49.030 --> 00:44:50.740
And that's now a small number.

00:44:50.740 --> 00:44:53.530
These are ongoing projects
getting started now.

00:44:53.530 --> 00:44:56.880
Genomics England has an
100,000 Genomes Project

00:44:56.880 --> 00:44:59.600
that was mentioned earlier
that they're getting going,

00:44:59.600 --> 00:45:01.640
with exactly this n equals many.

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:02.640
Let's learn.

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:05.370
And President Obama, when he
launched the Precision Medicine

00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:07.410
Initiative, exactly the
same insight-- let's

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:09.980
build a cohort of a
million volunteers,

00:45:09.980 --> 00:45:13.130
get information from them,
including genomic information,

00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:15.490
take all the kinds
of tools and analyses

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:19.506
we've learned about today,
and use it to learn from n

00:45:19.506 --> 00:45:21.670
equals many so that each
of us, when we need it,

00:45:21.670 --> 00:45:23.430
have the tools and
information needed

00:45:23.430 --> 00:45:25.485
to help us and our loved ones.

00:45:25.485 --> 00:45:29.180
I'd like to close with
thanking Kris and Ilya and Igor

00:45:29.180 --> 00:45:29.727
and Jason.

00:45:29.727 --> 00:45:31.060
And thank you all for listening.

00:45:31.060 --> 00:45:32.270
We're going to hang
around at the front

00:45:32.270 --> 00:45:33.440
if anyone has
questions afterward.

00:45:33.440 --> 00:45:34.093
Thank you.

00:45:34.093 --> 00:45:37.720
[APPLAUSE]

